            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3928/01477447-20160503-03</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 50-year-old woman with a chronic polyarthropathy was seen by her orthopedist for long-standing back and shoulder and worsening hip pain. A lateral labral tear and chronic trochanteric bursitis were diagnosed on hip magnetic resonance imaging, which was otherwise unremarkable. Hip arthroscopy was performed revealing an unusual bluish-tinged femoral head articular surface. Computed tomography scans of the spine were also obtained.</AbstractText>
